    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is understandable to feel frustrated when you have strong qualifications—a completed PhD, an established publication record, and teaching experience—and are passed over for a candidate who appears on paper to have fewer traditional academic metrics. \n\nHowever, looking at the situation objectively from both an academic hiring and procedural standpoint: **no, you should not make an issue of the fact that the panel was entirely female, nor is it on its own a sign of bias or impropriety.**\n\nHere is an analysis of why that is the case, what factors likely drove the decision, and how best to proceed.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition\n* **Institutional roles dictate panels:** Academic selection committees are typically appointed based on administrative roles (e.g., Head of Department, Director of Teaching, Search Committee Chair, HR representative, or relevant subject-matter leads) rather than demographic balancing. In departments where women hold several of these leadership or committee positions, a single-gender panel can easily occur by circumstance.\n* **Legal and evidentiary standards:** In employment and discrimination law, the demographic composition of a panel alone is not evidence of bias. Unless there were explicit discriminatory statements, breaches of university hiring policy, or demonstrable failure to follow the published rubric, alleging bias on demographic grounds has virtually no formal standing and carries significant professional reputation risk.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why Qualifications on Paper Diverge from Final Selection\nOnce an applicant reaches the shortlist of three, baseline academic qualification is already established for all candidates. At that final stage, hiring decisions rarely come down to pure metric counting (such as number of publications or years post-PhD). Other factors commonly outweigh raw counts:\n\n* **Strategic alignment and specialized need:** The announcement specifically highlighted \"strong experience from industry.\" If the department or business school needs someone to teach applied economics, liaise with industry partners, supervise internships, or attract corporate/policy placements, industry experience may have been weighted very heavily for this specific appointment.\n* **Research trajectory vs. volume:** Committees often evaluate the quality/ranking of the target journals, citation potential, and research pipeline rather than publication count alone. Hiring committees frequently appoint near-completion PhDs (ABDs) if their job talk and pipeline show high momentum.\n* **The job talk and interview performance:** Two rounds of interviews, job talks, and teaching demonstrations make up the bulk of final scoring. How well a candidate conveys teaching pedagogy, institutional fit, collegiality, and vision on the day often carries more weight than CV length.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\nTwo weeks without a reply to a request for written feedback is standard in higher education administration, not necessarily a sign of avoidance:\n* The appointment may still be conditional until formal contracts are signed and background checks are complete.\n* Public universities typically require HR to review or approve written feedback to unsuccessful shortlisted candidates.\n* Panel members have active teaching, administrative, and research commitments that delay post-interview paperwork.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Avoid raising gender or panel demographics:** Raising the gender composition of the panel without hard evidence of discrimination will not change the hiring outcome and can severely damage your standing within the academic community, which is relatively small and interconnected.\n2. **Follow up courteously on feedback:** Allow another week or two, then send a brief, professional follow-up email thanking them again for the interview opportunity and reiterating your interest in developmental feedback to aid future applications.\n3. **Review formal procedural channels only if there is concrete evidence:** If your university or jurisdiction has formal freedom of information (FOI) or public records mechanisms for scoring rubrics, or if an institutional ombudsman exists, those avenues exist to ensure procedural fairness. However, these should only be pursued if there was an actual procedural irregularity, not based on panel demographics.\n",
